{
  "dom_id": "step-q139282",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Consider the finite region bounded between the curves $y=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $y=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let $f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} & =-x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nNow, let's draw our usual vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower and upper functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224770/assets/27919b5cbe7c0d8b7f7c58a9378f5aa3d30e5466c63b1b17d8a1b6af028ecf66.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=g(x)=-x+\\frac{5}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=f(x)=2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x([\\text{upper}]-[\\text{lower}])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x([2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]-[-x+\\frac{5}{2}])\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}x(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{5}(-{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2{x}^{3}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =\\pi [(-\\frac{625}{4}+250-\\frac{125}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{4}+2-\\frac{5}{2})] \\\\  & =\\pi [\\frac{125}{4}-(-\\frac{3}{4})] \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
